rem $ Key to the European species of Psilocybe sect Psilocybe
1,2 $ Spores thin- or slightly thick-walled; lamellae of fresh and dried specimens ochre-brown to red-brown, lacking a purple-brown or chocolatebrown tinge (subsect. Inquilinae)
1,6 $ Spores with thickened walls; lamellae often dark purple-brown to chocolate- brown in fresh and dried specimens (subsect. Montanae)
2,3 $ Pileus viscid; pileipellis entirely peeling off as a hyaline, gelatinous pellicle; spores often more or less rhomboid or subangular in frontal view
2,4 $ Pileus dry; pileipellis not or only slightly peeling off; spores never tending to become rhomboid or subangular in frontal view (if so, compare  Psilocybe subhyperella Singer from S America, see type-studies below)
3,0 $ Spores large, in average 7.8-8.6 x 5.9-6.1 x 5.0-5.3 ^m, Q f = 1.4-1.5, Qs = 1.5-1.7; veil usually absent in mature fruitbodies; on dead leaves o f grasses 1. $ Psilocybe inquilinus
3,0 $ Spores small, in average 6.0-7.5 x 4.6-5.1 x 4.0-5.3 ¡¿m, Q f = 1.3-1.6 , Qs = 1.3-1.7; veil usually well-developed as appendiculate flocks at pileus margin and an annuliform zone and white fibrils on stipe; usually on woody substrates 2. $ Psilocybe crobula
4,5 $ Veil abundant, covering the young pileus entirely and visible as a cortina connecting the margin of the pileus with the stipe, leaving appendiculate patches at pileus margin in mature specimen; spores never strongly flattened, F/ I " 50 %
4,0 $ Veil usually only present as a cortina in young specimens, almost or entirely absent in mature specimens 5. $ Psilocybe subviscida var. subvisdda
5,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to oblong in side-view with small germ-pore; margin of pileus often grooved 3. $ Psilocybe castanella
5,0 $ Spores amygdaliform with apical papilla, with large germ-pore, often somewhat truncate; margin of pileus not grooved 4. $ Psilocybe velifera
6,7 $ Spores rhomboid, F/I up to 90 %
6,9 $ Spores not rhomboid, F/I up to 50 % or much lower
7,0 $ Pileus viscid, glabrous 6. $ Psilocybe phyllogena
7,8 $ Pileus dry, fibrillose to flocculose
8,0 $ Pleurocystidia present, similar to cheilocystidia 7. $ Psilocybe flocculosa
8,0 $ Pleurocystidia absent 8. $ Psilocybe cf. rhomboidospora
9,0 $ Spores without or with a very small germ-pore 9. $ Psilocybe micropora
9,10 $ Spores with a distinct germ-pore
10,0 $ Spores small, 5.5-7.0 x 4.0-5.0(-5.5) x 3.5-4.5 |im $ Psilocybe xeroderma
10,11 $ Spores larger
11,12 $ Spores 9.0-11.5 |im long
11,13 $ Spores 7.0-9.5 |im long
12,0 $ Pileus viscid with a thick, peeling pellicle, indistinctly translucently striate 11. $ Psilocybe pratensis
12,0 $ Pileus dry, pellis not peeling, translucently striate up to centre 12. $ Psilocybe montana var. macrospora
13,14 $ Veil absent, or only a fugaceous cortina present; usually among mosses on open, short vegetation types
13,15 $ Veil present as appendiculate patches at pileus margin and as fibrils and/or an annuliform zone on stipe; usually in mesic to eutrophic grasslands or in swampy areas among Juncus or Carex species
14,0 $ Spores in average 7.5-8.6 x 5.1-5.7 x 4.8-S.7 |im, Q f = 1.4-1.6 , slightly flattened, ovoid to mitriform in side-view, often truncate because of a large germ-pore; saprotrophic among mosses such as Polytrichum piliferum Hedw ., Racomitrium canescens (HEDW.) Brid . or Campylopus introflexus (HEDW.) Br id . 12. $ Psilocybe montana var. montana
14,0 $ Spores in average 7.3-8.4 x 4.9-5.5 x 5.7-5.9 nm, Q f = 1.3-1.55, flattened, appearing mitriform or slightly angular in frontal view, with medium-sized germ-pore; often in alpine snow-bed vegetation, parasitic on Polytrichum sexangulare FLORKE ex Br id . but also found in lowland Fagus forest 13. $ Psilocybe chionophila
15,0 $ Cheilocystidia 20-35 x 5.5-10 |im, often tibiiform, rarely lageniform, usually with globose 2 .0 -6 .0 |im wide capitulum; on damp, fertile soil in marshy places, wet meadows among Juncus or Carex, etc. 14. $ Psilocybe magica
15,0 $ Cheilocystidia 20-44 x 3.0-7.0 |im, lageniform or subcylindrical with attenuated, 1.5-3.0 jam wide neck; in groups on raw humus in fertilized grasslands, lawns, road-sides, also on rotten manure, straw or old dung 5. $ Psilocybe subviscida var. velataDescription of the accepted European species
